A||||7||N_Man||Crane cams break-in additive||||1||
Z||000000||N_Man||06-26-2000||12:54 AM||||with my compucam I got a bottle of oil additive for the breakin period.  They recomend using it and then change oil after 100 miles.<P>The big question is should I add it or just use plain oil.<P>I did a search on additives most people think there bad, But since this is for initial breakin only is it Ok.  Will it hurt anything?<P>TIA||||reg||1
Z||000001||2FAST4U||06-26-2000||01:13 AM||||By all means - use the break in addative. Also follow the initial start up instruction closely. If the instructions say to bring the revs up to 2,000 for the first 30 minutes do it. The initial startup period is the most important part of the whole installation and will determine how long the cam lasts. Also make sure to coat the lobes well with assembly lube. A coating of white grease on the bearing surfaces is also a good idea.<P>Good luck<P>Joe||||reg||4
Z||000002||batboy||06-26-2000||10:12 AM||||I don't know what is in the additive, but I'm assuming some sort of moly lubricant. That's what you usually get with a cam except in paste form. Often it's a real bright colored grease that is super sticky. You coat the lobes with it, I'm pretty sure you can buy this stuff in the auto parts store. I'd rather use the moly grease than an additive, if I had a choice. Even with the grease, you still have to change oil after a few hundred miles when breaking in an engine.||||reg||1
Z||000003||lowCG||06-26-2000||10:26 AM||||When breaking in a new camshaft,I found that it's good to use all you can get,any spiritual leaders that you can find to bless the engine wouldn't hurt either.<BR>The trick is have the engine fire up right away,so you can get the revs up to 2000 for the first half an hour,cranking over and over can score the cam's surfaces.<BR>You need to change the oil after,because the molly particles will clog up the filter.<BR>Good luck.||||reg||1
Z||000004||batboy||06-26-2000||07:32 PM||||There's nothing like a big block Chevy engine firing up for the first time. Thunderous, if you're running open headers at break in RPM. My ears always ring for a hour afterwards.||||reg||1
Z||000005||lowCG||06-26-2000||09:20 PM||||Yeah,big blocks sound like there's a string of fire crackers going off when they idle with open headers too.One time,at the track,I was using the sani-can,when this motor lit off;I couldn't believe that the walls of the sani-can were moving with the pulses of the 468 cube,mega compression,roller cammed rat motor.<BR>I think I need one of those,on a stand to just go and start up now and then.||||reg||1
Z||000006||ThatRickGuy||06-28-2000||12:17 PM||||blah cheby. The sounds of a Pontiac 450 during break in, open headers, wild cam, that's the greatest sound in the world  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000007||Phil||06-28-2000||12:25 PM||||The whine of a supercharger on a 3800 under accelleration sounding off just behind your seats is very pleasing also.||||reg||1
